WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.803
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit 6: People in Society.

2
00:00:03.883 --> 00:00:11.065
Unit 6 is about the people who keep a community
working: the helpers, the leaders, and the newcomers.

3
00:00:11.145 --> 00:00:19.477
You will meet a janitor, a carpenter, an engineer and a
merchant, then governors, senators, lawyers and the military,

4
00:00:19.557 --> 00:00:26.092
and finally artists, photographers, messengers,
refugees, immigrants, tenants and consumers.

5
00:00:26.172 --> 00:00:33.713
You will use the Present Simple to say what each person
does, should and mustn't to give advice and rules,

6
00:00:33.793 --> 00:00:39.537
prepositions to say where people go, and
the past tenses to retell what happened.

7
00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:47.662
By the end you will describe the helpers in your own town,
just as Amal does in the story “The Community Parade”.

8
00:00:47.742 --> 00:00:53.702
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:53.782 --> 00:00:55.630
By the end of this unit,

10
00:00:55.710 --> 00:01:03.422
you will be able to name and describe at least
twenty jobs and roles that people play in society.

11
00:01:03.502 --> 00:01:10.332
You will also explain what different workers
and leaders do, using the Present Simple.

12
00:01:10.412 --> 00:01:15.714
You will also give advice and state
rules using should and mustn't.

13
00:01:15.794 --> 00:01:18.687
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:25.294
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: janitor,
carpenter, labourer, engineer, merchant, governor.

15
00:01:25.374 --> 00:01:30.391
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:30.471 --> 00:01:34.355
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:34.435 --> 00:01:40.844
The reading sequence includes The People of Our
Town and Two Neighbours: A Refugee and an Immigrant.

18
00:01:40.924 --> 00:01:41.753
Read actively.

19
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:46.230
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:50.447
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:55.016
The first language focus is Present
Simple: describing everyday jobs.

22
00:01:55.096 --> 00:02:00.709
We use the Present Simple for routines,
facts, and things that happen again and again.

23
00:02:00.789 --> 00:02:07.793
It suits jobs perfectly, because most jobs repeat every
day: “The janitor cleans the school every morning.”

24
00:02:07.873 --> 00:02:15.009
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

25
00:02:15.089 --> 00:02:18.978
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Describe a Job.

26
00:02:19.058 --> 00:02:23.562
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

27
00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:27.051
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

28
00:02:27.131 --> 00:02:30.678
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

29
00:02:30.758 --> 00:02:32.342
You are ready to begin.

30
00:02:32.422 --> 00:02:39.503
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

31
00:02:39.583 --> 00:02:42.975
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

32
00:02:43.055 --> 00:02:51.005
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
